WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.251 --> 00:00:02.711
Hi, I'm Fred Shapiro.

00:00:02.711 --> 00:00:06.339
I'm the associate director of
the Law Library here at Yale.

00:00:06.673 --> 00:00:09.968
I'm also the editor of The New
Yale Book of Quotations,

00:00:10.301 --> 00:00:13.763
and I'm here to talk about
that book and talk about

00:00:13.763 --> 00:00:15.473
a few of the quotations that

00:00:15.473 --> 00:00:18.476
I particularly like or that are
particularly meaningful

00:00:18.476 --> 00:00:20.603
and why I selected them.

00:00:26.025 --> 00:00:29.529 align:start position:0%
Black Lives Matter,
obviously, is a very big quote

00:00:29.529 --> 00:00:31.656 align:start position:0%
in our political
and social life,

00:00:31.656 --> 00:00:32.741 align:start position:0%
and it was first

00:00:32.741 --> 00:00:36.536 align:start position:0%
coined by a writer and activist
named Alicia Garza.

00:00:36.828 --> 00:00:39.873 size:72% position:64%
We're living in a very
turbulent, crazy time,

00:00:40.457 --> 00:00:43.918 size:72% position:64%
which has generated
many remarkable quotes

00:00:43.918 --> 00:00:46.296
and Black Lives Matter. It's
a very short quote.

00:00:46.296 --> 00:00:48.298
It's really a slogan,

00:00:48.298 --> 00:00:51.342
but it's had such a
tremendous influence

00:00:51.342 --> 00:00:56.055
on our culture and
our politics in recent years

00:00:56.222 --> 00:00:59.476
that I think its merits
speak for themselves.

00:01:00.435 --> 00:01:03.271 align:start position:0%
"Believe those who are seeking
the truth, doubt

00:01:03.271 --> 00:01:06.775 align:start position:0%
those who find it." by
the French writer André Gide.

00:01:06.983 --> 00:01:08.735
This encapsulates

00:01:08.735 --> 00:01:11.112
the one of the big issues
of our time, which is

00:01:11.905 --> 00:01:16.576
truth and accuracy
versus fabrications.

00:01:17.118 --> 00:01:20.997
It addresses the phenomenon
which is so prevalent

00:01:20.997 --> 00:01:23.124
now of people that are

00:01:24.083 --> 00:01:27.796
fanatics or are narrowly
focused on a particular

00:01:29.005 --> 00:01:32.967 align:start position:0%
agenda. "Nobody loves
me but my mother,

00:01:33.259 --> 00:01:36.971
and she could be jivin' too."
by the musician B.B. King.

00:01:37.138 --> 00:01:40.308
There is a lot of humor in the
book, a lot of wit and humor.

00:01:40.642 --> 00:01:43.520
I particularly like this quote,
I'd like to include

00:01:43.520 --> 00:01:46.606
quotes from the arts,
from the music world.

00:01:47.148 --> 00:01:49.776
And this one, I just thought
was a particularly funny one.

00:01:51.361 --> 00:01:53.404 size:84% position:42%
"I am not smart, but genius

00:01:53.404 --> 00:01:57.575 size:81% position:41%
and a very stable genius
at that!" Donald J. Trump.

00:01:57.742 --> 00:01:59.786 align:start position:0%
This quote is amazing

00:01:59.953 --> 00:02:03.289 align:start position:0%
on many levels. This is someone
who many people feel

00:02:03.289 --> 00:02:07.502
is not smart and
not a genius and not stable.

00:02:08.294 --> 00:02:12.465
But yet he prides himself
on those those very attributes.

00:02:12.674 --> 00:02:15.343
This is not a book
that only has admirable,

00:02:15.677 --> 00:02:17.178
eloquent quotations.

00:02:17.178 --> 00:02:20.473
It has quotations
that are famous.

00:02:20.682 --> 00:02:23.643
That's one of the big criteria
for inclusion, quotations

00:02:23.643 --> 00:02:26.229
that are historically
important. And Mr.

00:02:26.229 --> 00:02:27.689
Trump has many of those.

00:02:29.691 --> 00:02:31.985 align:start position:0%
"There is nothing to be
learned from history anymore.

00:02:32.277 --> 00:02:35.488 align:start position:0%
We're in science fiction now."
by Allen Ginsberg.

00:02:35.572 --> 00:02:38.116
I know I personally
have the sense, but maybe many

00:02:38.783 --> 00:02:41.828
others have the sense that

00:02:41.828 --> 00:02:44.873
we've crossed some kind
of divide, that instead of

00:02:44.873 --> 00:02:46.875
watching postapocalyptic

00:02:46.875 --> 00:02:50.086
movies, maybe we're living
in a post apocalyptic movie,

00:02:51.045 --> 00:02:54.340
and that's what Ginsberg
was meaning to convey

00:02:54.716 --> 00:02:57.176
even a few decades ago
when he said that quote.

00:02:59.012 --> 00:03:01.055 align:start position:0%
"I don't want to
belong to any club

00:03:01.306 --> 00:03:05.101
that would accept me as one of
its members." by Groucho Marx,

00:03:05.101 --> 00:03:08.938 align:start position:0%
and this is a quote
that has a lot of psychological

00:03:10.481 --> 00:03:13.651
validity that many people feel.

00:03:15.904 --> 00:03:17.697 size:81% position:41%
"God grant me the serenity

00:03:17.697 --> 00:03:20.158 align:start position:0%
to accept the things
I cannot change,

00:03:20.158 --> 00:03:23.244 align:start position:0%
courage to change the things
I can, and the wisdom

00:03:23.494 --> 00:03:27.498
to know the difference." This
is the famous Serenity Prayer,

00:03:27.916 --> 00:03:32.545 size:78% position:39%
and it has a very complex
and rich origin story.

00:03:32.712 --> 00:03:35.173
It's usually attributed
to Reinhold Niebuhr,

00:03:35.173 --> 00:03:39.218
who was a great theologian and
a great political philosopher.

00:03:39.677 --> 00:03:41.679
I've done a lot of
research on this prayer,

00:03:41.721 --> 00:03:44.390
and almost 20 years
have been studying this.

00:03:44.807 --> 00:03:46.309
Niebuhr's daughter

00:03:46.309 --> 00:03:50.897
says that in 1943 was when her
father sat down and wrote it.

00:03:50.939 --> 00:03:55.818
But if you search newspaper
indexes, newspaper databases,

00:03:55.818 --> 00:04:00.198
you'll find that it was used
as far back as 1933.

00:04:00.198 --> 00:04:02.867
The earliest record
we have of someone using it was

00:04:03.993 --> 00:04:06.120
a woman named Winifred Weigel.

00:04:06.579 --> 00:04:10.750
An official of the YWCA wrote
The Serenity Prayer,

00:04:10.959 --> 00:04:14.837
we believe, and she's
the earliest known user of it.

00:04:15.505 --> 00:04:18.967
And she is a completely obscure

00:04:19.425 --> 00:04:23.054
woman. Reinhold Niebuhr
is a very, very famous man.

00:04:23.388 --> 00:04:26.265 size:88% position:44%
And this is actually
a pattern that I found

00:04:26.265 --> 00:04:29.060
in preparing my book, that
there were many famous quotes

00:04:29.352 --> 00:04:32.438
by obscure women
that are attributed

00:04:32.647 --> 00:04:34.691
to famous men, prominent men.

00:04:35.733 --> 00:04:39.570
And this, I think, is the most
interesting example of that,

00:04:39.570 --> 00:04:43.700
and in my book I go into some
detail in explaining the story,

00:04:43.700 --> 00:04:47.286
the true story of the Serenity
Prayer and its origins.

